{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016YJsDNDNvkYUG3KhcseNSr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'All four claims hold as written. Lead with ''28-30% instability\n2\t  persists in architecture-homogeneous decoder-only populations with good IRT fit\n3\t  (RMSEA=0.024)'' — eliminates the strongest reviewer objection preemptively. Instability\n4\t  decomposition strengthens Claim 1 by showing partial ρ=0.22 (p=0.012) after controlling\n5\t  domain accuracy improvement — DIF captures genuine item function change beyond domain-level\n6\t  capability gains. Non-uniform DIF check confirms MH adequacy (78% overlap with SIBTEST).\n7\t  Paper runs the original framing: ''psychometric analysis reveals structural benchmark\n8\t  problems that existing contamination validation methodology cannot detect.'' Architecture\n9\t  stratification (plan_010) and cross-benchmark GSM1K validation (plan_004, already\n10\t  done) are load-bearing complementary evidence. Strongest possible ICLR submission.\n11\t\n12\t  '\n13\timagined_experiments:\n14\t- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n15\t  plan_id: plan_010\n16\t  description: 'Run DIF within decoder-only subpopulation to test whether IRT model\n17\t    fit improves and temporal DIF persists when architecture heterogeneity is removed.\n18\t    Includes instability decomposition (partial ρ controlling domain accuracy) and\n19\t    non-uniform DIF robustness check (SIBTEST/logistic regression).\n20\t\n21\t    '\n22\t  expected_results:\n23\t    decoder_only_n_models: 3487\n24\t    decoder_only_n_ref_2023: 892\n25\t    decoder_only_n_foc_2024h1: 513\n26\t    decoder_only_temporal_c_pct: 29.1\n27\t    decoder_only_sanity_random_c_pct: 0.14\n28\t    full_population_irt_rmsea: 0.067\n29\t    full_population_irt_cfi: 0.874\n30\t    decoder_only_irt_rmsea: 0.024\n31\t    decoder_only_irt_cfi: 0.966\n32\t    decoder_only_irt_tli: 0.958\n33\t    decoder_only_irt_srmr: 0.031\n34\t    rmsea_improvement_pct: 64.2\n35\t    cfi_improvement: 0.092\n36\t    full_vs_decoder_only_flag_jaccard: 0.71\n37\t    partial_rho_controlling_domain_accuracy: 0.22\n38\t    partial_rho_p_value: 0.012\n39\t    zero_order_rho_domain_accuracy: 0.307\n40\t    variance_explained_by_accuracy_alone: 9.4\n41\t    residual_instability_pct: 20.5\n42\t    non_uniform_sibtest_n_subjects_tested: 8\n43\t    non_uniform_sibtest_c_flags: 2814\n44\t    non_uniform_mh_c_flags: 3627\n45\t    non_uniform_mh_overlap_pct: 78\n46\t    non_uniform_only_sibtest_pct: 4.2\n47\t    non_uniform_only_mh_pct: 26.2\n48\t    logistic_regression_r2_interaction_median: 0.008\n49\t  result_rationale: 'Decoder-only models share transformer architecture, tokenizer\n50\t    family, and training paradigm — removing encoder-decoder (T5, BART, UL2) and hybrid\n51\t    (Mamba, RWKV, Griffin) models eliminates the multidimensional ability structure\n52\t    that confounds unidimensional IRT. RMSEA drops from 0.067 to 0.024 because the\n53\t    latent trait is now genuinely closer to unidimensional within the decoder-only\n54\t    population — these models vary primarily along a single capability axis (scale\n55\t    × data × training quality). CFI jumps above 0.95, validating the measurement model.\n56\t    Despite this improved fit, temporal C% drops only modestly from 31.3% to 29.1%\n57\t    — architecture mixing contributed ~2pp of spurious DIF, but the remaining 29%\n58\t    is real signal. The partial ρ=0.22 (controlling domain accuracy improvement) stays\n59\t    significant because item function change is partially but not fully explained\n60\t    by \"models got better\": some items genuinely changed HOW they function (e.g.,\n61\t    distractor attractiveness shifted, required reasoning chain changed in difficulty)\n62\t    beyond the aggregate accuracy gain. The 78% SIBTEST-MH overlap confirms MH captures\n63\t    the dominant uniform DIF pattern — the 22% SIBTEST-only flags represent non-uniform\n64\t    effects (items where DIF magnitude varies by ability level), which are real but\n65\t    secondary. The low logistic regression R² for the interaction term (0.008 median)\n66\t    confirms non-uniform DIF is present but small in magnitude relative to uniform\n67\t    DIF.\n68\t\n69\t    '\n70\t  tables:\n71\t  - title: 'Table: IRT Fit Comparison — Full Population vs Decoder-Only'\n72\t    columns:\n73\t    - population\n74\t    - n_models\n75\t    - RMSEA\n76\t    - CFI\n77\t    - TLI\n78\t    - SRMR\n79\t    - temporal_C_pct\n80\t    rows:\n81\t    - - full\n82\t      - 5219\n83\t      - 0.067\n84\t      - 0.874\n85\t      - 0.851\n86\t      - 0.058\n87\t      - 31.3\n88\t    - - decoder_only\n89\t      - 3487\n90\t      - 0.024\n91\t      - 0.966\n92\t      - 0.958\n93\t      - 0.031\n94\t      - 29.1\n95\t    - - encoder_decoder\n96\t      - 412\n97\t      - 0.041\n98\t      - 0.921\n99\t      - 0.903\n100\t      - 0.044\n101\t      - 34.8\n102\t  - title: 'Table: Instability Decomposition'\n103\t    columns:\n104\t    - predictor\n105\t    - rho\n106\t    - p_value\n107\t    - variance_explained_pct\n108\t    rows:\n109\t    - - domain_accuracy_improvement\n110\t      - 0.307\n111\t      - 0.02\n112\t      - 9.4\n113\t    - - partial_rho_residual\n114\t      - 0.22\n115\t      - 0.012\n116\t      - 4.8\n117\t    - - total_temporal_c_variance\n118\t      - null\n119\t      - null\n120\t      - 100.0\n121\t  - title: 'Table: Non-Uniform DIF Overlap (8 subjects)'\n122\t    columns:\n123\t    - method\n124\t    - n_c_flags\n125\t    - overlap_with_mh_pct\n126\t    - unique_flags_pct\n127\t    rows:\n128\t    - - MH_standard\n129\t      - 3627\n130\t      - 100.0\n131\t      - 26.2\n132\t    - - SIBTEST\n133\t      - 2814\n134\t      - 78.0\n135\t      - 4.2\n136\t    - - logistic_regression\n137\t      - 2951\n138\t      - 81.3\n139\t      - 3.8\n140\t  figures_description:\n141\t  - 'Scatter plot: full-population RMSEA by subject vs decoder-only RMSEA by subject,\n142\t    showing systematic improvement across all 57 subjects with regression line'\n143\t  - 'Venn diagram: MH C-flags vs SIBTEST C-flags for 8 tested subjects, showing 78%\n144\t    overlap region'\n145\t  - 'Partial regression plot: temporal C% residuals vs domain accuracy improvement\n146\t    residuals, slope = partial ρ = 0.22'\n147\t  analysis: 'This is the single most important experiment for the paper. The RMSEA\n148\t    improvement from 0.067 to 0.024 validates the measurement model for the decoder-only\n149\t    population — reviewers cannot dismiss the DIF findings as IRT misfit artifacts.\n150\t    The persistence of 29% temporal DIF eliminates the strongest objection (\"it''s\n151\t    just architecture mixing\"). The instability decomposition is the key new contribution:\n152\t    ρ=0.307 honest disclosure becomes a strength when we show partial ρ=0.22 remains\n153\t    significant — \"yes, capability improvement explains ~9% of variance in item instability,\n154\t    but ~5% is genuine item function change independent of aggregate accuracy gains.\"\n155\t    The non-uniform DIF check is a clean robustness confirmation — 78% overlap means\n156\t    MH captures the dominant signal, and the paper can state \"MH is adequate for this\n157\t    population; non-uniform effects are present but secondary.\"\n158\t\n159\t    '\n160\t- name: rl_robustness_validation\n161\t  plan_id: plan_002\n162\t  description: 'Empirically validate DIF stability across SFT-only, alignment-RL,\n163\t    and reasoning-RL cohort compositions. Compare DIF group-level robustness against\n164\t    traditional detector model-level degradation. Power analysis for detection boundary.\n165\t\n166\t    '\n167\t  expected_results:\n168\t    sft_only_n_models: 1843\n169\t    sft_only_temporal_c_pct: 30.8\n170\t    alignment_rl_n_models: 477\n171\t    alignment_rl_temporal_c_pct: 32.1\n172\t    reasoning_rl_n_models: 13\n173\t    reasoning_rl_models_added_to_focal: true\n174\t    cohort_with_rl_temporal_c_pct: 31.0\n175\t    flag_retention_sft_vs_alignment: 0.91\n176\t    flag_retention_sft_vs_with_rl: 0.89\n177\t    flag_retention_bootstrap_ci_95:\n178\t    - 0.84\n179\t    - 0.94\n180\t    constat_auroc_non_rl: 0.73\n181\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.54\n182\t    constat_auroc_drop: 0.19\n183\t    mink_auroc_non_rl: 0.68\n184\t    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.51\n185\t    dif_flag_stability_auroc: 0.88\n186\t    delong_dif_vs_constat_p: 0.003\n187\t    delong_dif_vs_mink_p: 0.008\n188\t    tost_equivalence_dif_retention_p: 0.021\n189\t    power_boundary_p_at_delta14: 0.45\n190\t    power_boundary_p_at_delta10: 0.62\n191\t    detection_tpr_at_p04: 0.38\n192\t    detection_tpr_at_p06: 0.61\n193\t    detection_tpr_at_p08: 0.79\n194\t    detection_tpr_at_p10: 0.92\n195\t  result_rationale: 'DIF is a group-level statistic computed over entire cohorts —\n196\t    adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a focal group of ~500 changes cohort composition\n197\t    by <3%, producing negligible perturbation to the MH statistic. Flag retention\n198\t    at 89-91% reflects minor sampling variability, not RL-induced degradation. Traditional\n199\t    detectors (ConStat, Min-K%) operate at the individual model level — reasoning-RL\n200\t    training with PPO/GRPO contracts the NLL gap between memorized and non-memorized\n201\t    items (Wang et al.), directly degrading these detectors'' signal. AUROC drops\n202\t    from 0.73/0.68 to 0.54/0.51 because the per-model distributional signature that\n203\t    these methods rely on is explicitly optimized away by RL. DIF''s immunity stems\n204\t    from its design: it conditions on total score and compares GROUP response patterns,\n205\t    making it agnostic to individual model training procedures. The power analysis\n206\t    shows detection boundary at p≈0.45 for δ=1.4 — below this exploitation rate, the\n207\t    contamination-induced DIF effect size drops below the MH detection threshold.\n208\t    This is an honest limitation: DIF catches strong differential contamination, not\n209\t    subtle partial exploitation.\n210\t\n211\t    '\n212\t  tables:\n213\t  - title: 'Table: DIF Flag Stability Across Cohort Compositions'\n214\t    columns:\n215\t    - cohort\n216\t    - n_models\n217\t    - temporal_c_pct\n218\t    - flag_retention_vs_sft\n219\t    - bootstrap_95ci\n220\t    rows:\n221\t    - - sft_only\n222\t      - 1843\n223\t      - 30.8\n224\t      - 1.0\n225\t      - —\n226\t    - - alignment_rl\n227\t      - 477\n228\t      - 32.1\n229\t      - 0.91\n230\t      - '[0.87, 0.95]'\n231\t    - - sft_plus_reasoning_rl\n232\t      - 1856\n233\t      - 31.0\n234\t      - 0.89\n235\t      - '[0.84, 0.94]'\n236\t    - - all_combined\n237\t      - 2333\n238\t      - 31.4\n239\t      - 0.9\n240\t      - '[0.85, 0.94]'\n241\t  - title: 'Table: Traditional Detector Degradation on Reasoning-RL Models'\n242\t    columns:\n243\t    - detector\n244\t    - auroc_non_rl\n245\t    - auroc_reasoning_rl\n246\t    - delta_auroc\n247\t    - delong_p\n248\t    rows:\n249\t    - - ConStat\n250\t      - 0.73\n251\t      - 0.54\n252\t      - -0.19\n253\t      - 0.003\n254\t    - - Min-K%\n255\t      - 0.68\n256\t      - 0.51\n257\t      - -0.17\n258\t      - 0.008\n259\t    - - DIF_group_flag\n260\t      - 0.88\n261\t      - 0.86\n262\t      - -0.02\n263\t      - 0.71\n264\t  figures_description:\n265\t  - 'Detection power curve: TPR vs exploitation rate p ∈ [0.3, 1.0] at δ=1.0, 1.4,\n266\t    1.6 showing detection boundary'\n267\t  - 'Paired AUROC comparison: DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K% on non-RL and reasoning-RL\n268\t    models with bootstrap CIs'\n269\t  analysis: 'Results confirm DIF''s architectural advantage: group-level statistics\n270\t    are inherently robust to individual model training variations. The N=13 reasoning-RL\n271\t    limitation is honestly acknowledged — bootstrap CIs on flag retention are wide\n272\t    [0.84, 0.94]. However, the DeLong tests are significant (p<0.01) for DIF vs traditional\n273\t    detectors, and TOST equivalence confirms DIF retention is statistically equivalent\n274\t    across compositions (p=0.021). This goes into the paper as supporting evidence\n275\t    for Claim 1''s robustness, with explicit caveats about N=13 in limitations. The\n276\t    power analysis provides a useful operational characterization: DIF works for strong\n277\t    differential contamination (p≥0.45), complementary methods needed for subtle effects.\n278\t\n279\t    '\n280\t- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n281\t  plan_id: plan_003a\n282\t  description: 'Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items\n283\t    to characterize detection power by effect size and RL training type.\n284\t\n285\t    '\n286\t  expected_results:\n287\t    n_items_reformulated: 237\n288\t    n_models_non_rl: 42\n289\t    n_models_reasoning_rl: 11\n290\t    constat_fdr_q005_flagged_pct: 17.3\n291\t    constat_auroc_non_rl_verbatim: 0.74\n292\t    constat_auroc_non_rl_variant: 0.61\n293\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl_verbatim: 0.55\n294\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl_variant: 0.48\n295\t    constat_tpr_at_fpr010_non_rl: 0.42\n296\t    constat_tpr_at_fpr010_rl: 0.18\n297\t    reformulation_semantic_similarity_mean: 0.91\n298\t    reformulation_difficulty_correlation: 0.83\n299\t    constat_dif_flag_jaccard: 0.18\n300\t  result_rationale: 'ConStat detects contamination by measuring performance DROP on\n301\t    semantically equivalent reformulations — if a model memorized the exact wording,\n302\t    it performs worse on paraphrases. This works well on non-RL models (AUROC=0.74)\n303\t    because memorization creates a sharp original-vs-reformulation gap. On reasoning-RL\n304\t    models, PPO/GRPO training generalizes the memorized knowledge to nearby phrasings,\n305\t    collapsing the perturbation signal (AUROC drops to 0.55). The 17.3% flagging rate\n306\t    at FDR q=0.05 is reasonable — ConStat is conservative by design. Low Jaccard overlap\n307\t    with DIF (0.18) is expected: ConStat detects per-model memorization signals while\n308\t    DIF detects group-level differential functioning — they capture fundamentally\n309\t    different aspects of contamination. Variant contamination (model trained on paraphrased\n310\t    items) is harder for ConStat (AUROC=0.61) because both original and reformulation\n311\t    are equally \"seen.\"\n312\t\n313\t    '\n314\t  tables:\n315\t  - title: 'Table: ConStat Detection Power by Model Type and Contamination Type'\n316\t    columns:\n317\t    - model_type\n318\t    - contam_type\n319\t    - auroc\n320\t    - tpr_at_fpr010\n321\t    - n_models\n322\t    rows:\n323\t    - - non_rl\n324\t      - verbatim\n325\t      - 0.74\n326\t      - 0.42\n327\t      - 42\n328\t    - - non_rl\n329\t      - variant\n330\t      - 0.61\n331\t      - 0.24\n332\t      - 42\n333\t    - - reasoning_rl\n334\t      - verbatim\n335\t      - 0.55\n336\t      - 0.18\n337\t      - 11\n338\t    - - reasoning_rl\n339\t      - variant\n340\t      - 0.48\n341\t      - 0.09\n342\t      - 11\n343\t  figures_description:\n344\t  - 'ROC curves: ConStat detection for non-RL vs reasoning-RL models, verbatim vs\n345\t    variant contamination'\n346\t  - 'Heatmap: ConStat flag vs DIF flag overlap by MMLU subject'\n347\t  analysis: 'ConStat provides a complementary signal to DIF: per-model perturbation\n348\t    sensitivity vs group-level differential functioning. Under best tier, ConStat\n349\t    works well enough on non-RL models to serve as an ensemble component, but degrades\n350\t    on reasoning-RL as expected. This experiment is marked as future work in the current\n351\t    paper framing — results would strengthen a methods-comparison table but are not\n352\t    load-bearing for any current claim.\n353\t\n354\t    '\n355\t- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n356\t  plan_id: plan_003b\n357\t  description: 'DVD generation variance analysis on 10-15 open-weight models with\n358\t    50 responses per item via temperature sampling.\n359\t\n360\t    '\n361\t  expected_results:\n362\t    n_models: 12\n363\t    n_items_tested: 500\n364\t    responses_per_item: 50\n365\t    temperature: 0.8\n366\t    dvd_bimodal_detection_pct: 12.4\n367\t    dvd_auroc_verbatim: 0.67\n368\t    dvd_auroc_variant: 0.53\n369\t    dvd_tpr_at_fpr010: 0.28\n370\t    generation_variance_clean_mean: 0.34\n371\t    generation_variance_contaminated_mean: 0.18\n372\t    variance_ratio_clean_vs_contam: 1.89\n373\t    dvd_dif_flag_jaccard: 0.13\n374\t    dvd_constat_flag_jaccard: 0.22\n375\t    gpu_hours_actual: 58\n376\t  result_rationale: 'DVD detects contamination via generation variance: memorized\n377\t    items produce consistently correct answers across temperature samples (low variance),\n378\t    while genuinely understood items show natural stochasticity. The 1.89 variance\n379\t    ratio is moderate — contaminated items have roughly half the generation variance\n380\t    of clean items. 12.4% bimodal detection reflects items where some models show\n381\t    memorization signature (near-zero variance) while others show normal variance.\n382\t    AUROC=0.67 for verbatim contamination is decent but below ConStat (0.74) because\n383\t    generation variance is a noisier signal — temperature sampling introduces variance\n384\t    even on memorized items, and some genuinely easy items also have low variance.\n385\t    Variant contamination AUROC drops to 0.53 (near chance) because paraphrase-based\n386\t    contamination doesn''t produce the sharp variance reduction that verbatim memorization\n387\t    does. Low Jaccard with DIF (0.13) is expected — DVD is a per-model metric while\n388\t    DIF is group-level.\n389\t\n390\t    '\n391\t  tables:\n392\t  - title: 'Table: DVD Generation Variance Statistics'\n393\t    columns:\n394\t    - item_category\n395\t    - mean_variance\n396\t    - std_variance\n397\t    - bimodal_pct\n398\t    rows:\n399\t    - - clean_items\n400\t      - 0.34\n401\t      - 0.12\n402\t      - 3.1\n403\t    - - dif_flagged_items\n404\t      - 0.29\n405\t      - 0.15\n406\t      - 8.7\n407\t    - - injected_verbatim\n408\t      - 0.18\n409\t      - 0.09\n410\t      - 12.4\n411\t    - - injected_variant\n412\t      - 0.31\n413\t      - 0.13\n414\t      - 5.2\n415\t  figures_description:\n416\t  - 'Violin plot: generation variance distribution by item category (clean, DIF-flagged,\n417\t    injected)'\n418\t  - 'Bimodality detection example: variance histogram for a representative contaminated\n419\t    item showing bimodal peak structure'\n420\t  analysis: 'DVD provides the weakest individual signal of the three methods but captures\n421\t    a unique dimension — generation-time behavioral signatures that neither DIF nor\n422\t    ConStat access. As with plan_003a, this is future work under current framing.\n423\t    The 58 GPU-hours cost is substantial for a supporting result. Strongest contribution\n424\t    would be in the ensemble (plan_003c) where DVD covers variant contamination cases\n425\t    that ConStat misses.\n426\t\n427\t    '\n428\t- name: ensemble_fusion\n429\t  plan_id: plan_003c\n430\t  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with calibrated\n431\t    thresholds, majority voting, and weighted fusion.\n432\t\n433\t    '\n434\t  expected_results:\n435\t    majority_2of3_fpr: 0.07\n436\t    majority_2of3_tpr_verbatim: 0.58\n437\t    majority_2of3_tpr_variant: 0.34\n438\t    weighted_fusion_fpr: 0.06\n439\t    weighted_fusion_tpr_verbatim: 0.64\n440\t    weighted_fusion_tpr_variant: 0.39\n441\t    weighted_fusion_auroc: 0.82\n442\t    uniform_weight_auroc: 0.79\n443\t    cv_5fold_auroc_std: 0.03\n444\t    dif_coverage_delta_ge_14: true\n445\t    constat_coverage_non_rl_delta_ge_06: true\n446\t    dvd_coverage_variant: partial\n447\t    ensemble_fpr_all_configs_below_010: true\n448\t    optimal_weights:\n449\t      dif: 0.45\n450\t      constat: 0.35\n451\t      dvd: 0.2\n452\t  result_rationale: 'The ensemble works because the three detectors exploit orthogonal\n453\t    signals: DIF uses group-level response pattern differences (robust to RL, needs\n454\t    large groups), ConStat uses per-model perturbation sensitivity (strong on non-RL,\n455\t    degrades on RL), DVD uses generation variance (unique signal, noisiest). Majority\n456\t    voting at 2-of-3 achieves FPR=0.07 because false positives from one method are\n457\t    unlikely to be corroborated by another operating on a different signal. The weighted\n458\t    fusion gives DIF the highest weight (0.45) because it has the best calibrated\n459\t    FPR and strongest theoretical grounding. Cross-validation shows stable performance\n460\t    (std=0.03 on AUROC) because the three signals are largely uncorrelated in their\n461\t    error patterns. The ensemble FPR < 0.10 across all configurations confirms operational\n462\t    reliability. Variant contamination remains the hardest case (TPR=0.39) because\n463\t    no individual method handles it well — this is an honest limitation.\n464\t\n465\t    '\n466\t  tables:\n467\t  - title: 'Table: Ensemble Performance Comparison'\n468\t    columns:\n469\t    - strategy\n470\t    - fpr\n471\t    - tpr_verbatim\n472\t    - tpr_variant\n473\t    - auroc\n474\t    rows:\n475\t    - - dif_only\n476\t      - 0.04\n477\t      - 0.48\n478\t      - 0.12\n479\t      - 0.76\n480\t    - - constat_only\n481\t      - 0.06\n482\t      - 0.42\n483\t      - 0.24\n484\t      - 0.71\n485\t    - - dvd_only\n486\t      - 0.08\n487\t      - 0.28\n488\t      - 0.19\n489\t      - 0.64\n490\t    - - majority_2of3\n491\t      - 0.07\n492\t      - 0.58\n493\t      - 0.34\n494\t      - 0.79\n495\t    - - weighted_fusion\n496\t      - 0.06\n497\t      - 0.64\n498\t      - 0.39\n499\t      - 0.82\n500\t    - - uniform_weight\n501\t      - 0.07\n502\t      - 0.61\n503\t      - 0.36\n504\t      - 0.79\n505\t  figures_description:\n506\t  - 'Coverage Venn diagram: items detected by DIF-only, ConStat-only, DVD-only, and\n507\t    overlaps'\n508\t  - 'ROC curves: individual detectors vs majority voting vs weighted fusion'\n509\t  analysis: 'The ensemble demonstrates clear additive value over any single detector.\n510\t    Under best tier, the +0.06 AUROC gain from DIF-only (0.76) to weighted fusion\n511\t    (0.82) justifies the multi-method approach. However, this entire experiment tree\n512\t    (003a/b/c) is currently designated as future work — the paper''s four claims stand\n513\t    without it. Strongest case for inclusion would be as a §6 \"Extensions\" section\n514\t    showing the framework''s composability. The 270 total GPU-hours across 003a/b/c\n515\t    is a significant investment for a non-load-bearing result.\n516\t\n517\t    '\n518\t- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n519\t  plan_id: plan_005\n520\t  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU: fit bifactor model, select anchor\n521\t    items, calibrate replacements via fixed-parameter calibration, demonstrate equated\n522\t    score equivalence.\n523\t\n524\t    '\n525\t  expected_results:\n526\t    bifactor_general_factor_variance_pct: 61.3\n527\t    bifactor_subject_factor_variance_pct_range:\n528\t    - 8.2\n529\t    - 27.4\n530\t    bifactor_rmsea: 0.019\n531\t    bifactor_cfi: 0.978\n532\t    n_anchor_items_selected: 6107\n533\t    anchor_selection_criteria: no_DIF_flag AND discrimination>0.5 AND fit_residual<2.0\n534\t    anchor_pct_of_total: 48.8\n535\t    n_replacement_items_calibrated: 500\n536\t    fixed_param_calibration_convergence_pct: 94.2\n537\t    equated_score_spearman_rho: 0.976\n538\t    equated_score_rmse: 1.83\n539\t    equated_vs_unequated_rank_change_max: 127\n540\t    equated_vs_unequated_rank_change_mean: 8.4\n541\t  result_rationale: 'The bifactor model fits well (RMSEA=0.019, CFI=0.978) because\n542\t    MMLU genuinely has a general factor (broad LLM capability) plus subject-specific\n543\t    factors — this is the correct psychometric model for a multidimensional benchmark.\n544\t    The general factor explains 61% of variance, confirming that a dominant unidimensional\n545\t    signal exists (supporting the adequacy of simpler IRT for ranking purposes). Anchor\n546\t    item selection retains ~49% of items — the non-DIF items with adequate discrimination\n547\t    and model fit. This is enough for stable equating. Fixed-parameter calibration\n548\t    converges at 94% because most replacement items are well-behaved (the 6% non-convergence\n549\t    reflects items with extreme parameters or poor fit). The equated score ρ=0.976\n550\t    exceeds the 0.95 threshold because equating preserves the dominant general factor\n551\t    — subject-specific variance introduces minor rank perturbations (mean change 8.4\n552\t    ranks out of ~5000) but the overall ordering is highly stable. This confirms MMLU''s\n553\t    measurement redundancy finding from plan_017 via a different lens.\n554\t\n555\t    '\n556\t  tables:\n557\t  - title: 'Table: Bifactor Model Fit and Factor Structure'\n558\t    columns:\n559\t    - factor\n560\t    - variance_explained_pct\n561\t    - n_items_loading_above_03\n562\t    rows:\n563\t    - - general\n564\t      - 61.3\n565\t      - 12508\n566\t    - - subject_specific_mean\n567\t      - 15.8\n568\t      - 219\n569\t    - - subject_specific_min\n570\t      - 8.2\n571\t      - 87\n572\t    - - subject_specific_max\n573\t      - 27.4\n574\t      - 412\n575\t  - title: 'Table: Equating Quality by Replacement Rate'\n576\t    columns:\n577\t    - replacement_pct\n578\t    - n_replaced\n579\t    - spearman_rho\n580\t    - rmse\n581\t    - max_rank_change\n582\t    rows:\n583\t    - - 5\n584\t      - 625\n585\t      - 0.998\n586\t      - 0.41\n587\t      - 23\n588\t    - - 10\n589\t      - 1251\n590\t      - 0.994\n591\t      - 0.89\n592\t      - 54\n593\t    - - 20\n594\t      - 2502\n595\t      - 0.987\n596\t      - 1.34\n597\t      - 89\n598\t    - - 31\n599\t      - 3918\n600\t      - 0.976\n601\t      - 1.83\n602\t      - 127\n603\t  figures_description:\n604\t  - 'Scree plot: eigenvalues for general factor + top 10 subject factors, showing\n605\t    dominant first eigenvalue'\n606\t  - 'Bland-Altman plot: equated vs original scores, showing mean bias and limits of\n607\t    agreement'\n608\t  analysis: 'This experiment extends plan_017''s ranking redundancy finding by providing\n609\t    a principled psychometric framework for item replacement. Under best tier, the\n610\t    ρ=0.976 result at 31% replacement (replacing ALL DIF items) confirms that equated\n611\t    benchmarks can maintain validity. However, this experiment relates to the dropped\n612\t    \"regeneration\" claim (old Claim 3) — under the current paper pivot, this is future\n613\t    work. Could appear as a brief §7 \"Toward Benchmark Maintenance\" sketch if space\n614\t    permits.\n615\t\n616\t    '\n617\t- name: item_generation_calibration\n618\t  plan_id: plan_006\n619\t  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM\n620\t    generation with construct constraints, calibrated via student simulation.\n621\t\n622\t    '\n623\t  expected_results:\n624\t    n_dif_items_targeted: 500\n625\t    n_replacements_generated: 1500\n626\t    n_replacements_passing_quality: 1127\n627\t    quality_pass_rate: 0.751\n628\t    student_sim_difficulty_correlation: 0.78\n629\t    prompt_based_difficulty_correlation: 0.32\n630\t    student_sim_discrimination_correlation: 0.64\n631\t    empirical_difficulty_match_within_015_logits: 0.68\n632\t    empirical_difficulty_match_within_030_logits: 0.89\n633\t    holdout_panel_n_models: 30\n634\t    holdout_spearman_original_vs_replacement: 0.971\n635\t    subject_coverage_all_57: true\n636\t    cognitive_level_match_pct: 82.4\n637\t    gpu_hours_actual: 173\n638\t  result_rationale: 'LLM-generated items achieve r=0.78 difficulty correlation via\n639\t    student simulation because the simulation approach (arXiv 2601.09953) directly\n640\t    estimates how a population of models will respond — much more informative than\n641\t    prompt-based difficulty targeting (r=0.32), which only captures surface-level\n642\t    difficulty cues. The 75% quality pass rate reflects that ~25% of generated items\n643\t    have flaws: ambiguous wording, incorrect answer keys, or construct misalignment\n644\t    detected by automated checks. The 0.68 rate of difficulty matching within 0.15\n645\t    logits means most replacements are \"close enough\" for equating — the remaining\n646\t    32% need iterative regeneration or manual adjustment. 82% cognitive level match\n647\t    indicates LLMs can reproduce factual recall and comprehension items well, but\n648\t    struggle more with application and analysis items (the 18% mismatch concentrates\n649\t    in these higher Bloom levels). The holdout ρ=0.971 confirms replacement items\n650\t    preserve measurement function at the benchmark level.\n651\t\n652\t    '\n653\t  tables:\n654\t  - title: 'Table: Difficulty Calibration Method Comparison'\n655\t    columns:\n656\t    - method\n657\t    - difficulty_r\n658\t    - discrimination_r\n659\t    - within_015_logits_pct\n660\t    rows:\n661\t    - - prompt_based_targeting\n662\t      - 0.32\n663\t      - 0.18\n664\t      - 0.24\n665\t    - - student_simulation\n666\t      - 0.78\n667\t      - 0.64\n668\t      - 0.68\n669\t    - - student_sim_plus_iterative\n670\t      - 0.84\n671\t      - 0.71\n672\t      - 0.82\n673\t  figures_description:\n674\t  - 'Scatter plot: predicted difficulty (student sim) vs empirical difficulty, with\n675\t    r=0.78 annotation'\n676\t  - 'Bar chart: cognitive level match rates by Bloom''s taxonomy level (remember,\n677\t    understand, apply, analyze)'\n678\t  analysis: 'Item generation calibration demonstrates a practical pipeline for benchmark\n679\t    maintenance. Under best tier, the student simulation approach substantially outperforms\n680\t    naive prompt-based generation. However, 173 GPU-hours and the dependency on plan_005\n681\t    make this the most resource-intensive experiment chain. Like plan_005, this supports\n682\t    the dropped regeneration claim — future work under current framing.\n683\t\n684\t    '\n685\t- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n686\t  plan_id: plan_007\n687\t  description: 'Simulate 4 successive benchmark administrations with cumulative Bayesian\n688\t    DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) to show power improvement from evidence accumulation.\n689\t\n690\t    '\n691\t  expected_results:\n692\t    n_simulated_administrations: 4\n693\t    n_items_per_administration: 12508\n694\t    n_models_per_administration:\n695\t    - 1253\n696\t    - 656\n697\t    - 800\n698\t    - 900\n699\t    individual_power_delta_10: 0.41\n700\t    cumulative_power_2_admin_delta_10: 0.57\n701\t    cumulative_power_3_admin_delta_10: 0.71\n702\t    cumulative_power_4_admin_delta_10: 0.77\n703\t    individual_fpr: 0.048\n704\t    cumulative_fpr_4_admin: 0.022\n705\t    bayesian_shrinkage_factor_mean: 0.73\n706\t    posterior_credible_interval_width_reduction_pct: 38\n707\t    individual_power_delta_14: 0.68\n708\t    cumulative_power_3_admin_delta_14: 0.89\n709\t  result_rationale: 'Bayesian DIF accumulates evidence across administrations via\n710\t    informative priors — each administration''s posterior becomes the next administration''s\n711\t    prior. At δ=1.0 (medium effect), individual administration power is only 0.41\n712\t    because the MH statistic has limited sensitivity at this effect size with typical\n713\t    LLM benchmark sample sizes. After 3 administrations, the accumulated evidence\n714\t    pushes power to 0.71 — the prior effectively increases the sample size by pooling\n715\t    information. FPR decreases from 0.048 to 0.022 because Bayesian shrinkage pulls\n716\t    spurious flags toward zero across administrations (transient false positives don''t\n717\t    persist). The 38% credible interval width reduction reflects genuine information\n718\t    gain. At δ=1.4 (the MH operational threshold from power simulations), cumulative\n719\t    power reaches 0.89 after 3 administrations — near the 0.90 monitoring standard.\n720\t    This provides a practical framework for longitudinal benchmark monitoring: any\n721\t    single time point may miss medium DIF, but persistent effects are reliably detected.\n722\t\n723\t    '\n724\t  tables:\n725\t  - title: 'Table: Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power by Administration Count'\n726\t    columns:\n727\t    - n_administrations\n728\t    - power_delta_08\n729\t    - power_delta_10\n730\t    - power_delta_14\n731\t    - fpr\n732\t    rows:\n733\t    - - 1\n734\t      - 0.22\n735\t      - 0.41\n736\t      - 0.68\n737\t      - 0.048\n738\t    - - 2\n739\t      - 0.35\n740\t      - 0.57\n741\t      - 0.81\n742\t      - 0.033\n743\t    - - 3\n744\t      - 0.47\n745\t      - 0.71\n746\t      - 0.89\n747\t      - 0.025\n748\t    - - 4\n749\t      - 0.54\n750\t      - 0.77\n751\t      - 0.93\n752\t      - 0.022\n753\t  figures_description:\n754\t  - 'Power curves: detection power vs effect size (δ) for 1-4 cumulative administrations,\n755\t    showing progressive improvement'\n756\t  - 'FPR trajectory: false positive rate decreasing across administrations due to\n757\t    Bayesian shrinkage'\n758\t  analysis: 'Cumulative Bayesian DIF is a natural extension for benchmark monitoring.\n759\t    Under best tier, the power improvement from 0.41 to 0.71 at δ=1.0 after 3 administrations\n760\t    is compelling for longitudinal deployment. Zero GPU cost makes this attractive.\n761\t    However, it''s P2 priority — a nice-to-have for a future-work section or a follow-up\n762\t    paper on operational benchmark monitoring. The simulation is clean and fast to\n763\t    execute; if space permits, a single figure could convey the key message.\n764\t\n765\t    '\n766\t- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n767\t  plan_id: plan_008\n768\t  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement:\n769\t    define validity metrics, simulate replacement rates 5-50%, establish theoretical\n770\t    bounds.\n771\t\n772\t    '\n773\t  expected_results:\n774\t    replacement_5pct_rho: 0.9991\n775\t    replacement_5pct_shr: 0.9928\n776\t    replacement_5pct_construct_coverage: 0.997\n777\t    replacement_10pct_rho: 0.9984\n778\t    replacement_10pct_shr: 0.9921\n779\t    replacement_10pct_construct_coverage: 0.993\n780\t    replacement_20pct_rho: 0.9962\n781\t    replacement_20pct_shr: 0.9904\n782\t    replacement_20pct_construct_coverage: 0.984\n783\t    replacement_50pct_rho: 0.9871\n784\t    replacement_50pct_shr: 0.9842\n785\t    replacement_50pct_construct_coverage: 0.951\n786\t    theoretical_bound_rho: 1 - O(r × (1 - ρ_anchor²))\n787\t    theoretical_bound_shr: 1 - O(r × σ²_replacement / σ²_original)\n788\t    critical_replacement_rate_rho_below_099: 0.27\n789\t    critical_replacement_rate_shr_below_098: 0.41\n790\t  result_rationale: 'MMLU''s massive measurement redundancy (ρ=0.997 at 31% removal\n791\t    from plan_017) means replacement degrades validity very slowly. At 5% replacement,\n792\t    ρ=0.9991 because only 625 items change, and equating ensures replacement items\n793\t    measure similar constructs. The degradation is approximately linear in replacement\n794\t    rate when equating is well- calibrated, because each replaced item introduces\n795\t    independent small perturbation. The theoretical bound involves the replacement\n796\t    rate r multiplied by the (1 - ρ²_anchor) term — when anchor items are strong (ρ_anchor\n797\t    ≈ 0.99), the multiplicative penalty is tiny. SHR degrades slightly faster than\n798\t    Spearman ρ because split-half reliability is more sensitive to local item perturbations\n799\t    than rank ordering. Construct coverage (proportion of subject domains with ≥10\n800\t    items remaining) stays above 0.95 even at 50% replacement because MMLU''s 57 subjects\n801\t    average 219 items each — even halving doesn''t eliminate coverage. The critical\n802\t    rate of 27% for ρ<0.99 aligns with the empirical finding that 31% DIF removal\n803\t    gives ρ=0.996 — replacement with equated items is LESS disruptive than removal.\n804\t\n805\t    '\n806\t  tables:\n807\t  - title: 'Table: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement'\n808\t    columns:\n809\t    - replacement_pct\n810\t    - spearman_rho\n811\t    - shr\n812\t    - construct_coverage\n813\t    - max_rank_change\n814\t    rows:\n815\t    - - 0\n816\t      - 1.0\n817\t      - 0.9934\n818\t      - 1.0\n819\t      - 0\n820\t    - - 5\n821\t      - 0.9991\n822\t      - 0.9928\n823\t      - 0.997\n824\t      - 18\n825\t    - - 10\n826\t      - 0.9984\n827\t      - 0.9921\n828\t      - 0.993\n829\t      - 42\n830\t    - - 20\n831\t      - 0.9962\n832\t      - 0.9904\n833\t      - 0.984\n834\t      - 78\n835\t    - - 31\n836\t      - 0.9938\n837\t      - 0.9886\n838\t      - 0.972\n839\t      - 127\n840\t    - - 50\n841\t      - 0.9871\n842\t      - 0.9842\n843\t      - 0.951\n844\t      - 203\n845\t  figures_description:\n846\t  - 'Line plot: Spearman ρ and SHR vs replacement rate (0-50%), with theoretical bound\n847\t    overlay'\n848\t  - 'Heatmap: per-subject construct coverage at each replacement rate, highlighting\n849\t    subjects dropping below 10 items'\n850\t  analysis: 'This experiment provides the theoretical backbone for equating-based\n851\t    benchmark maintenance. Under best tier, the key message is that MMLU can tolerate\n852\t    ~27% item replacement while maintaining ρ>0.99 — and since DIF flags 31% of items,\n853\t    a single replacement cycle could refresh the most unstable items with minimal\n854\t    validity loss. Depends on plan_005/006; shares their \"future work\" status under\n855\t    current framing.\n856\t\n857\t    '\n858\t- name: item_focused_trees\n859\t  plan_id: plan_009\n860\t  description: 'Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) using model release\n861\t    date as continuous covariate. Compare detection power and flexibility vs categorical\n862\t    DIF.\n863\t\n864\t    '\n865\t  expected_results:\n866\t    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n867\t    n_models_with_release_date: 3842\n868\t    ift_significant_date_effect_pct: 34.7\n869\t    ift_gradual_drift_items_pct: 8.3\n870\t    ift_abrupt_change_items_pct: 26.4\n871\t    ift_categorical_dif_overlap_pct: 81.2\n872\t    ift_unique_detections_pct: 5.8\n873\t    categorical_dif_unique_pct: 2.4\n874\t    ift_split_date_mode: 2023-09\n875\t    ift_split_date_range:\n876\t    - 2023-06\n877\t    - 2024-01\n878\t    ift_detection_power_delta_14: 0.72\n879\t    ift_fpr: 0.041\n880\t    ift_vs_mh_concordance_kappa: 0.74\n881\t  result_rationale: 'IFT treats release date as a continuous covariate and recursively\n882\t    partitions the model sample to find the split that maximizes DIF detection — avoiding\n883\t    the arbitrary 2023/2024-H1 boundary. The 81% overlap with categorical DIF confirms\n884\t    the temporal boundary is approximately correct: IFT''s modal split date (2023-09)\n885\t    is close to the categorical cutoff. The 5.8% unique IFT detections are items with\n886\t    gradual drift — their DIF effect accumulates slowly over time, crossing the threshold\n887\t    only when IFT optimally places the split point. These tend to be items where capability\n888\t    improvement is continuous rather than punctuated (e.g., math reasoning items that\n889\t    gradually become easier). The 8.3% gradual drift items (where IFT fits a smooth\n890\t    function rather than a step change) capture a distinct drift pattern: these items\n891\t    don''t suddenly change but their functioning shifts continuously as model capability\n892\t    increases. IFT detection power (0.72 at δ=1.4) is comparable to MH (0.68) because\n893\t    optimal split placement partially compensates for the loss of predetermined grouping.\n894\t    Cohen''s κ=0.74 indicates substantial agreement — the two methods capture largely\n895\t    overlapping phenomena via different mechanisms.\n896\t\n897\t    '\n898\t  tables:\n899\t  - title: 'Table: IFT vs Categorical DIF Detection Comparison'\n900\t    columns:\n901\t    - method\n902\t    - significant_pct\n903\t    - unique_pct\n904\t    - fpr\n905\t    - power_delta_14\n906\t    - kappa\n907\t    rows:\n908\t    - - categorical_mh\n909\t      - 31.3\n910\t      - 2.4\n911\t      - 0.042\n912\t      - 0.68\n913\t      - —\n914\t    - - ift_continuous\n915\t      - 34.7\n916\t      - 5.8\n917\t      - 0.041\n918\t      - 0.72\n919\t      - 0.74\n920\t    - - intersection\n921\t      - 28.9\n922\t      - —\n923\t      - —\n924\t      - —\n925\t      - —\n926\t  - title: 'Table: IFT Drift Pattern Classification'\n927\t    columns:\n928\t    - pattern\n929\t    - pct_of_significant\n930\t    - example_domain\n931\t    - interpretation\n932\t    rows:\n933\t    - - abrupt_change\n934\t      - 76.1\n935\t      - abstract_algebra\n936\t      - sudden capability jump at specific model generation\n937\t    - - gradual_drift\n938\t      - 23.9\n939\t      - high_school_mathematics\n940\t      - continuous capability improvement over time\n941\t  figures_description:\n942\t  - 'IFT tree visualization for a representative item: split at 2023-09, showing DIF\n943\t    effect size in each partition'\n944\t  - 'Temporal DIF effect trajectory: 5 example items showing abrupt vs gradual drift\n945\t    patterns'\n946\t  analysis: IFT validates the categorical DIF approach while revealing additional\n947\t    nuance. Under best tier, the 81% overlap confirms the 2023/2024-H1 split is appropriate,\n948\t    and the unique gradual-drift detections provide a richer characterization of temporal\n949\t    instability. P2 priority — an elegant methodological refinement that strengthens\n950\t    Claim 1 but isn't required for it. The key insight (gradual vs abrupt drift patterns)\n951\t    could motivate a follow-up paper on continuous benchmark monitoring. Zero GPU\n952\t    cost; the main investment is implementation of the IFT algorithm.\n953\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5d5edfa-9e75-4e61-963f-f1abacb60b8f","uuid":"9eebe2c9-37cf-4123-b211-c8dc77719764","timestamp":"2026-05-21T03:54:56.568Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032826/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'All four claims hold as written. Lead with ''28-30% instability\n  persists in architecture-homogeneous decoder-only populations with good IRT fit\n  (RMSEA=0.024)'' — eliminates the strongest reviewer objection preemptively. Instability\n  decomposition strengthens Claim 1 by showing partial ρ=0.22 (p=0.012) after controlling\n  domain accuracy improvement — DIF captures genuine item function change beyond domain-level\n  capability gains. Non-uniform DIF check confirms MH adequacy (78% overlap with SIBTEST).\n  Paper runs the original framing: ''psychometric analysis reveals structural benchmark\n  problems that existing contamination validation methodology cannot detect.'' Architecture\n  stratification (plan_010) and cross-benchmark GSM1K validation (plan_004, already\n  done) are load-bearing complementary evidence. Strongest possible ICLR submission.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Run DIF within decoder-only subpopulation to test whether IRT model\n    fit improves and temporal DIF persists when architecture heterogeneity is removed.\n    Includes instability decomposition (partial ρ controlling domain accuracy) and\n    non-uniform DIF robustness check (SIBTEST/logistic regression).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    decoder_only_n_models: 3487\n    decoder_only_n_ref_2023: 892\n    decoder_only_n_foc_2024h1: 513\n    decoder_only_temporal_c_pct: 29.1\n    decoder_only_sanity_random_c_pct: 0.14\n    full_population_irt_rmsea: 0.067\n    full_population_irt_cfi: 0.874\n    decoder_only_irt_rmsea: 0.024\n    decoder_only_irt_cfi: 0.966\n    decoder_only_irt_tli: 0.958\n    decoder_only_irt_srmr: 0.031\n    rmsea_improvement_pct: 64.2\n    cfi_improvement: 0.092\n    full_vs_decoder_only_flag_jaccard: 0.71\n    partial_rho_controlling_domain_accuracy: 0.22\n    partial_rho_p_value: 0.012\n    zero_order_rho_domain_accuracy: 0.307\n    variance_explained_by_accuracy_alone: 9.4\n    residual_instability_pct: 20.5\n    non_uniform_sibtest_n_subjects_tested: 8\n    non_uniform_sibtest_c_flags: 2814\n    non_uniform_mh_c_flags: 3627\n    non_uniform_mh_overlap_pct: 78\n    non_uniform_only_sibtest_pct: 4.2\n    non_uniform_only_mh_pct: 26.2\n    logistic_regression_r2_interaction_median: 0.008\n  result_rationale: 'Decoder-only models share transformer architecture, tokenizer\n    family, and training paradigm — removing encoder-decoder (T5, BART, UL2) and hybrid\n    (Mamba, RWKV, Griffin) models eliminates the multidimensional ability structure\n    that confounds unidimensional IRT. RMSEA drops from 0.067 to 0.024 because the\n    latent trait is now genuinely closer to unidimensional within the decoder-only\n    population — these models vary primarily along a single capability axis (scale\n    × data × training quality). CFI jumps above 0.95, validating the measurement model.\n    Despite this improved fit, temporal C% drops only modestly from 31.3% to 29.1%\n    — architecture mixing contributed ~2pp of spurious DIF, but the remaining 29%\n    is real signal. The partial ρ=0.22 (controlling domain accuracy improvement) stays\n    significant because item function change is partially but not fully explained\n    by \"models got better\": some items genuinely changed HOW they function (e.g.,\n    distractor attractiveness shifted, required reasoning chain changed in difficulty)\n    beyond the aggregate accuracy gain. The 78% SIBTEST-MH overlap confirms MH captures\n    the dominant uniform DIF pattern — the 22% SIBTEST-only flags represent non-uniform\n    effects (items where DIF magnitude varies by ability level), which are real but\n    secondary. The low logistic regression R² for the interaction term (0.008 median)\n    confirms non-uniform DIF is present but small in magnitude relative to uniform\n    DIF.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: IRT Fit Comparison — Full Population vs Decoder-Only'\n    columns:\n    - population\n    - n_models\n    - RMSEA\n    - CFI\n    - TLI\n    - SRMR\n    - temporal_C_pct\n    rows:\n    - - full\n      - 5219\n      - 0.067\n      - 0.874\n      - 0.851\n      - 0.058\n      - 31.3\n    - - decoder_only\n      - 3487\n      - 0.024\n      - 0.966\n      - 0.958\n      - 0.031\n      - 29.1\n    - - encoder_decoder\n      - 412\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.921\n      - 0.903\n      - 0.044\n      - 34.8\n  - title: 'Table: Instability Decomposition'\n    columns:\n    - predictor\n    - rho\n    - p_value\n    - variance_explained_pct\n    rows:\n    - - domain_accuracy_improvement\n      - 0.307\n      - 0.02\n      - 9.4\n    - - partial_rho_residual\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.012\n      - 4.8\n    - - total_temporal_c_variance\n      - null\n      - null\n      - 100.0\n  - title: 'Table: Non-Uniform DIF Overlap (8 subjects)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - n_c_flags\n    - overlap_with_mh_pct\n    - unique_flags_pct\n    rows:\n    - - MH_standard\n      - 3627\n      - 100.0\n      - 26.2\n    - - SIBTEST\n      - 2814\n      - 78.0\n      - 4.2\n    - - logistic_regression\n      - 2951\n      - 81.3\n      - 3.8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: full-population RMSEA by subject vs decoder-only RMSEA by subject,\n    showing systematic improvement across all 57 subjects with regression line'\n  - 'Venn diagram: MH C-flags vs SIBTEST C-flags for 8 tested subjects, showing 78%\n    overlap region'\n  - 'Partial regression plot: temporal C% residuals vs domain accuracy improvement\n    residuals, slope = partial ρ = 0.22'\n  analysis: 'This is the single most important experiment for the paper. The RMSEA\n    improvement from 0.067 to 0.024 validates the measurement model for the decoder-only\n    population — reviewers cannot dismiss the DIF findings as IRT misfit artifacts.\n    The persistence of 29% temporal DIF eliminates the strongest objection (\"it''s\n    just architecture mixing\"). The instability decomposition is the key new contribution:\n    ρ=0.307 honest disclosure becomes a strength when we show partial ρ=0.22 remains\n    significant — \"yes, capability improvement explains ~9% of variance in item instability,\n    but ~5% is genuine item function change independent of aggregate accuracy gains.\"\n    The non-uniform DIF check is a clean robustness confirmation — 78% overlap means\n    MH captures the dominant signal, and the paper can state \"MH is adequate for this\n    population; non-uniform effects are present but secondary.\"\n\n    '\n- name: rl_robustness_validation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Empirically validate DIF stability across SFT-only, alignment-RL,\n    and reasoning-RL cohort compositions. Compare DIF group-level robustness against\n    traditional detector model-level degradation. Power analysis for detection boundary.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    sft_only_n_models: 1843\n    sft_only_temporal_c_pct: 30.8\n    alignment_rl_n_models: 477\n    alignment_rl_temporal_c_pct: 32.1\n    reasoning_rl_n_models: 13\n    reasoning_rl_models_added_to_focal: true\n    cohort_with_rl_temporal_c_pct: 31.0\n    flag_retention_sft_vs_alignment: 0.91\n    flag_retention_sft_vs_with_rl: 0.89\n    flag_retention_bootstrap_ci_95:\n    - 0.84\n    - 0.94\n    constat_auroc_non_rl: 0.73\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.54\n    constat_auroc_drop: 0.19\n    mink_auroc_non_rl: 0.68\n    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.51\n    dif_flag_stability_auroc: 0.88\n    delong_dif_vs_constat_p: 0.003\n    delong_dif_vs_mink_p: 0.008\n    tost_equivalence_dif_retention_p: 0.021\n    power_boundary_p_at_delta14: 0.45\n    power_boundary_p_at_delta10: 0.62\n    detection_tpr_at_p04: 0.38\n    detection_tpr_at_p06: 0.61\n    detection_tpr_at_p08: 0.79\n    detection_tpr_at_p10: 0.92\n  result_rationale: 'DIF is a group-level statistic computed over entire cohorts —\n    adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a focal group of ~500 changes cohort composition\n    by <3%, producing negligible perturbation to the MH statistic. Flag retention\n    at 89-91% reflects minor sampling variability, not RL-induced degradation. Traditional\n    detectors (ConStat, Min-K%) operate at the individual model level — reasoning-RL\n    training with PPO/GRPO contracts the NLL gap between memorized and non-memorized\n    items (Wang et al.), directly degrading these detectors'' signal. AUROC drops\n    from 0.73/0.68 to 0.54/0.51 because the per-model distributional signature that\n    these methods rely on is explicitly optimized away by RL. DIF''s immunity stems\n    from its design: it conditions on total score and compares GROUP response patterns,\n    making it agnostic to individual model training procedures. The power analysis\n    shows detection boundary at p≈0.45 for δ=1.4 — below this exploitation rate, the\n    contamination-induced DIF effect size drops below the MH detection threshold.\n    This is an honest limitation: DIF catches strong differential contamination, not\n    subtle partial exploitation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: DIF Flag Stability Across Cohort Compositions'\n    columns:\n    - cohort\n    - n_models\n    - temporal_c_pct\n    - flag_retention_vs_sft\n    - bootstrap_95ci\n    rows:\n    - - sft_only\n      - 1843\n      - 30.8\n      - 1.0\n      - —\n    - - alignment_rl\n      - 477\n      - 32.1\n      - 0.91\n      - '[0.87, 0.95]'\n    - - sft_plus_reasoning_rl\n      - 1856\n      - 31.0\n      - 0.89\n      - '[0.84, 0.94]'\n    - - all_combined\n      - 2333\n      - 31.4\n      - 0.9\n      - '[0.85, 0.94]'\n  - title: 'Table: Traditional Detector Degradation on Reasoning-RL Models'\n    columns:\n    - detector\n    - auroc_non_rl\n    - auroc_reasoning_rl\n    - delta_auroc\n    - delong_p\n    rows:\n    - - ConStat\n      - 0.73\n      - 0.54\n      - -0.19\n      - 0.003\n    - - Min-K%\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.51\n      - -0.17\n      - 0.008\n    - - DIF_group_flag\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.86\n      - -0.02\n      - 0.71\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Detection power curve: TPR vs exploitation rate p ∈ [0.3, 1.0] at δ=1.0, 1.4,\n    1.6 showing detection boundary'\n  - 'Paired AUROC comparison: DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K% on non-RL and reasoning-RL\n    models with bootstrap CIs'\n  analysis: 'Results confirm DIF''s architectural advantage: group-level statistics\n    are inherently robust to individual model training variations. The N=13 reasoning-RL\n    limitation is honestly acknowledged — bootstrap CIs on flag retention are wide\n    [0.84, 0.94]. However, the DeLong tests are significant (p<0.01) for DIF vs traditional\n    detectors, and TOST equivalence confirms DIF retention is statistically equivalent\n    across compositions (p=0.021). This goes into the paper as supporting evidence\n    for Claim 1''s robustness, with explicit caveats about N=13 in limitations. The\n    power analysis provides a useful operational characterization: DIF works for strong\n    differential contamination (p≥0.45), complementary methods needed for subtle effects.\n\n    '\n- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003a\n  description: 'Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items\n    to characterize detection power by effect size and RL training type.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_reformulated: 237\n    n_models_non_rl: 42\n    n_models_reasoning_rl: 11\n    constat_fdr_q005_flagged_pct: 17.3\n    constat_auroc_non_rl_verbatim: 0.74\n    constat_auroc_non_rl_variant: 0.61\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl_verbatim: 0.55\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl_variant: 0.48\n    constat_tpr_at_fpr010_non_rl: 0.42\n    constat_tpr_at_fpr010_rl: 0.18\n    reformulation_semantic_similarity_mean: 0.91\n    reformulation_difficulty_correlation: 0.83\n    constat_dif_flag_jaccard: 0.18\n  result_rationale: 'ConStat detects contamination by measuring performance DROP on\n    semantically equivalent reformulations — if a model memorized the exact wording,\n    it performs worse on paraphrases. This works well on non-RL models (AUROC=0.74)\n    because memorization creates a sharp original-vs-reformulation gap. On reasoning-RL\n    models, PPO/GRPO training generalizes the memorized knowledge to nearby phrasings,\n    collapsing the perturbation signal (AUROC drops to 0.55). The 17.3% flagging rate\n    at FDR q=0.05 is reasonable — ConStat is conservative by design. Low Jaccard overlap\n    with DIF (0.18) is expected: ConStat detects per-model memorization signals while\n    DIF detects group-level differential functioning — they capture fundamentally\n    different aspects of contamination. Variant contamination (model trained on paraphrased\n    items) is harder for ConStat (AUROC=0.61) because both original and reformulation\n    are equally \"seen.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: ConStat Detection Power by Model Type and Contamination Type'\n    columns:\n    - model_type\n    - contam_type\n    - auroc\n    - tpr_at_fpr010\n    - n_models\n    rows:\n    - - non_rl\n      - verbatim\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.42\n      - 42\n    - - non_rl\n      - variant\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.24\n      - 42\n    - - reasoning_rl\n      - verbatim\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.18\n      - 11\n    - - reasoning_rl\n      - variant\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.09\n      - 11\n  figures_description:\n  - 'ROC curves: ConStat detection for non-RL vs reasoning-RL models, verbatim vs\n    variant contamination'\n  - 'Heatmap: ConStat flag vs DIF flag overlap by MMLU subject'\n  analysis: 'ConStat provides a complementary signal to DIF: per-model perturbation\n    sensitivity vs group-level differential functioning. Under best tier, ConStat\n    works well enough on non-RL models to serve as an ensemble component, but degrades\n    on reasoning-RL as expected. This experiment is marked as future work in the current\n    paper framing — results would strengthen a methods-comparison table but are not\n    load-bearing for any current claim.\n\n    '\n- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003b\n  description: 'DVD generation variance analysis on 10-15 open-weight models with\n    50 responses per item via temperature sampling.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_models: 12\n    n_items_tested: 500\n    responses_per_item: 50\n    temperature: 0.8\n    dvd_bimodal_detection_pct: 12.4\n    dvd_auroc_verbatim: 0.67\n    dvd_auroc_variant: 0.53\n    dvd_tpr_at_fpr010: 0.28\n    generation_variance_clean_mean: 0.34\n    generation_variance_contaminated_mean: 0.18\n    variance_ratio_clean_vs_contam: 1.89\n    dvd_dif_flag_jaccard: 0.13\n    dvd_constat_flag_jaccard: 0.22\n    gpu_hours_actual: 58\n  result_rationale: 'DVD detects contamination via generation variance: memorized\n    items produce consistently correct answers across temperature samples (low variance),\n    while genuinely understood items show natural stochasticity. The 1.89 variance\n    ratio is moderate — contaminated items have roughly half the generation variance\n    of clean items. 12.4% bimodal detection reflects items where some models show\n    memorization signature (near-zero variance) while others show normal variance.\n    AUROC=0.67 for verbatim contamination is decent but below ConStat (0.74) because\n    generation variance is a noisier signal — temperature sampling introduces variance\n    even on memorized items, and some genuinely easy items also have low variance.\n    Variant contamination AUROC drops to 0.53 (near chance) because paraphrase-based\n    contamination doesn''t produce the sharp variance reduction that verbatim memorization\n    does. Low Jaccard with DIF (0.13) is expected — DVD is a per-model metric while\n    DIF is group-level.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: DVD Generation Variance Statistics'\n    columns:\n    - item_category\n    - mean_variance\n    - std_variance\n    - bimodal_pct\n    rows:\n    - - clean_items\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.12\n      - 3.1\n    - - dif_flagged_items\n      - 0.29\n      - 0.15\n      - 8.7\n    - - injected_verbatim\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.09\n      - 12.4\n    - - injected_variant\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.13\n      - 5.2\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Violin plot: generation variance distribution by item category (clean, DIF-flagged,\n    injected)'\n  - 'Bimodality detection example: variance histogram for a representative contaminated\n    item showing bimodal peak structure'\n  analysis: 'DVD provides the weakest individual signal of the three methods but captures\n    a unique dimension — generation-time behavioral signatures that neither DIF nor\n    ConStat access. As with plan_003a, this is future work under current framing.\n    The 58 GPU-hours cost is substantial for a supporting result. Strongest contribution\n    would be in the ensemble (plan_003c) where DVD covers variant contamination cases\n    that ConStat misses.\n\n    '\n- name: ensemble_fusion\n  plan_id: plan_003c\n  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with calibrated\n    thresholds, majority voting, and weighted fusion.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    majority_2of3_fpr: 0.07\n    majority_2of3_tpr_verbatim: 0.58\n    majority_2of3_tpr_variant: 0.34\n    weighted_fusion_fpr: 0.06\n    weighted_fusion_tpr_verbatim: 0.64\n    weighted_fusion_tpr_variant: 0.39\n    weighted_fusion_auroc: 0.82\n    uniform_weight_auroc: 0.79\n    cv_5fold_auroc_std: 0.03\n    dif_coverage_delta_ge_14: true\n    constat_coverage_non_rl_delta_ge_06: true\n    dvd_coverage_variant: partial\n    ensemble_fpr_all_configs_below_010: true\n    optimal_weights:\n      dif: 0.45\n      constat: 0.35\n      dvd: 0.2\n  result_rationale: 'The ensemble works because the three detectors exploit orthogonal\n    signals: DIF uses group-level response pattern differences (robust to RL, needs\n    large groups), ConStat uses per-model perturbation sensitivity (strong on non-RL,\n    degrades on RL), DVD uses generation variance (unique signal, noisiest). Majority\n    voting at 2-of-3 achieves FPR=0.07 because false positives from one method are\n    unlikely to be corroborated by another operating on a different signal. The weighted\n    fusion gives DIF the highest weight (0.45) because it has the best calibrated\n    FPR and strongest theoretical grounding. Cross-validation shows stable performance\n    (std=0.03 on AUROC) because the three signals are largely uncorrelated in their\n    error patterns. The ensemble FPR < 0.10 across all configurations confirms operational\n    reliability. Variant contamination remains the hardest case (TPR=0.39) because\n    no individual method handles it well — this is an honest limitation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Ensemble Performance Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - strategy\n    - fpr\n    - tpr_verbatim\n    - tpr_variant\n    - auroc\n    rows:\n    - - dif_only\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.12\n      - 0.76\n    - - constat_only\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.71\n    - - dvd_only\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.64\n    - - majority_2of3\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.79\n    - - weighted_fusion\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.39\n      - 0.82\n    - - uniform_weight\n      - 0.07\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.36\n      - 0.79\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Coverage Venn diagram: items detected by DIF-only, ConStat-only, DVD-only, and\n    overlaps'\n  - 'ROC curves: individual detectors vs majority voting vs weighted fusion'\n  analysis: 'The ensemble demonstrates clear additive value over any single detector.\n    Under best tier, the +0.06 AUROC gain from DIF-only (0.76) to weighted fusion\n    (0.82) justifies the multi-method approach. However, this entire experiment tree\n    (003a/b/c) is currently designated as future work — the paper''s four claims stand\n    without it. Strongest case for inclusion would be as a §6 \"Extensions\" section\n    showing the framework''s composability. The 270 total GPU-hours across 003a/b/c\n    is a significant investment for a non-load-bearing result.\n\n    '\n- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU: fit bifactor model, select anchor\n    items, calibrate replacements via fixed-parameter calibration, demonstrate equated\n    score equivalence.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    bifactor_general_factor_variance_pct: 61.3\n    bifactor_subject_factor_variance_pct_range:\n    - 8.2\n    - 27.4\n    bifactor_rmsea: 0.019\n    bifactor_cfi: 0.978\n    n_anchor_items_selected: 6107\n    anchor_selection_criteria: no_DIF_flag AND discrimination>0.5 AND fit_residual<2.0\n    anchor_pct_of_total: 48.8\n    n_replacement_items_calibrated: 500\n    fixed_param_calibration_convergence_pct: 94.2\n    equated_score_spearman_rho: 0.976\n    equated_score_rmse: 1.83\n    equated_vs_unequated_rank_change_max: 127\n    equated_vs_unequated_rank_change_mean: 8.4\n  result_rationale: 'The bifactor model fits well (RMSEA=0.019, CFI=0.978) because\n    MMLU genuinely has a general factor (broad LLM capability) plus subject-specific\n    factors — this is the correct psychometric model for a multidimensional benchmark.\n    The general factor explains 61% of variance, confirming that a dominant unidimensional\n    signal exists (supporting the adequacy of simpler IRT for ranking purposes). Anchor\n    item selection retains ~49% of items — the non-DIF items with adequate discrimination\n    and model fit. This is enough for stable equating. Fixed-parameter calibration\n    converges at 94% because most replacement items are well-behaved (the 6% non-convergence\n    reflects items with extreme parameters or poor fit). The equated score ρ=0.976\n    exceeds the 0.95 threshold because equating preserves the dominant general factor\n    — subject-specific variance introduces minor rank perturbations (mean change 8.4\n    ranks out of ~5000) but the overall ordering is highly stable. This confirms MMLU''s\n    measurement redundancy finding from plan_017 via a different lens.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Bifactor Model Fit and Factor Structure'\n    columns:\n    - factor\n    - variance_explained_pct\n    - n_items_loading_above_03\n    rows:\n    - - general\n      - 61.3\n      - 12508\n    - - subject_specific_mean\n      - 15.8\n      - 219\n    - - subject_specific_min\n      - 8.2\n      - 87\n    - - subject_specific_max\n      - 27.4\n      - 412\n  - title: 'Table: Equating Quality by Replacement Rate'\n    columns:\n    - replacement_pct\n    - n_replaced\n    - spearman_rho\n    - rmse\n    - max_rank_change\n    rows:\n    - - 5\n      - 625\n      - 0.998\n      - 0.41\n      - 23\n    - - 10\n      - 1251\n      - 0.994\n      - 0.89\n      - 54\n    - - 20\n      - 2502\n      - 0.987\n      - 1.34\n      - 89\n    - - 31\n      - 3918\n      - 0.976\n      - 1.83\n      - 127\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scree plot: eigenvalues for general factor + top 10 subject factors, showing\n    dominant first eigenvalue'\n  - 'Bland-Altman plot: equated vs original scores, showing mean bias and limits of\n    agreement'\n  analysis: 'This experiment extends plan_017''s ranking redundancy finding by providing\n    a principled psychometric framework for item replacement. Under best tier, the\n    ρ=0.976 result at 31% replacement (replacing ALL DIF items) confirms that equated\n    benchmarks can maintain validity. However, this experiment relates to the dropped\n    \"regeneration\" claim (old Claim 3) — under the current paper pivot, this is future\n    work. Could appear as a brief §7 \"Toward Benchmark Maintenance\" sketch if space\n    permits.\n\n    '\n- name: item_generation_calibration\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM\n    generation with construct constraints, calibrated via student simulation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_dif_items_targeted: 500\n    n_replacements_generated: 1500\n    n_replacements_passing_quality: 1127\n    quality_pass_rate: 0.751\n    student_sim_difficulty_correlation: 0.78\n    prompt_based_difficulty_correlation: 0.32\n    student_sim_discrimination_correlation: 0.64\n    empirical_difficulty_match_within_015_logits: 0.68\n    empirical_difficulty_match_within_030_logits: 0.89\n    holdout_panel_n_models: 30\n    holdout_spearman_original_vs_replacement: 0.971\n    subject_coverage_all_57: true\n    cognitive_level_match_pct: 82.4\n    gpu_hours_actual: 173\n  result_rationale: 'LLM-generated items achieve r=0.78 difficulty correlation via\n    student simulation because the simulation approach (arXiv 2601.09953) directly\n    estimates how a population of models will respond — much more informative than\n    prompt-based difficulty targeting (r=0.32), which only captures surface-level\n    difficulty cues. The 75% quality pass rate reflects that ~25% of generated items\n    have flaws: ambiguous wording, incorrect answer keys, or construct misalignment\n    detected by automated checks. The 0.68 rate of difficulty matching within 0.15\n    logits means most replacements are \"close enough\" for equating — the remaining\n    32% need iterative regeneration or manual adjustment. 82% cognitive level match\n    indicates LLMs can reproduce factual recall and comprehension items well, but\n    struggle more with application and analysis items (the 18% mismatch concentrates\n    in these higher Bloom levels). The holdout ρ=0.971 confirms replacement items\n    preserve measurement function at the benchmark level.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Difficulty Calibration Method Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - difficulty_r\n    - discrimination_r\n    - within_015_logits_pct\n    rows:\n    - - prompt_based_targeting\n      - 0.32\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.24\n    - - student_simulation\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.68\n    - - student_sim_plus_iterative\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.82\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: predicted difficulty (student sim) vs empirical difficulty, with\n    r=0.78 annotation'\n  - 'Bar chart: cognitive level match rates by Bloom''s taxonomy level (remember,\n    understand, apply, analyze)'\n  analysis: 'Item generation calibration demonstrates a practical pipeline for benchmark\n    maintenance. Under best tier, the student simulation approach substantially outperforms\n    naive prompt-based generation. However, 173 GPU-hours and the dependency on plan_005\n    make this the most resource-intensive experiment chain. Like plan_005, this supports\n    the dropped regeneration claim — future work under current framing.\n\n    '\n- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Simulate 4 successive benchmark administrations with cumulative Bayesian\n    DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) to show power improvement from evidence accumulation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_simulated_administrations: 4\n    n_items_per_administration: 12508\n    n_models_per_administration:\n    - 1253\n    - 656\n    - 800\n    - 900\n    individual_power_delta_10: 0.41\n    cumulative_power_2_admin_delta_10: 0.57\n    cumulative_power_3_admin_delta_10: 0.71\n    cumulative_power_4_admin_delta_10: 0.77\n    individual_fpr: 0.048\n    cumulative_fpr_4_admin: 0.022\n    bayesian_shrinkage_factor_mean: 0.73\n    posterior_credible_interval_width_reduction_pct: 38\n    individual_power_delta_14: 0.68\n    cumulative_power_3_admin_delta_14: 0.89\n  result_rationale: 'Bayesian DIF accumulates evidence across administrations via\n    informative priors — each administration''s posterior becomes the next administration''s\n    prior. At δ=1.0 (medium effect), individual administration power is only 0.41\n    because the MH statistic has limited sensitivity at this effect size with typical\n    LLM benchmark sample sizes. After 3 administrations, the accumulated evidence\n    pushes power to 0.71 — the prior effectively increases the sample size by pooling\n    information. FPR decreases from 0.048 to 0.022 because Bayesian shrinkage pulls\n    spurious flags toward zero across administrations (transient false positives don''t\n    persist). The 38% credible interval width reduction reflects genuine information\n    gain. At δ=1.4 (the MH operational threshold from power simulations), cumulative\n    power reaches 0.89 after 3 administrations — near the 0.90 monitoring standard.\n    This provides a practical framework for longitudinal benchmark monitoring: any\n    single time point may miss medium DIF, but persistent effects are reliably detected.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power by Administration Count'\n    columns:\n    - n_administrations\n    - power_delta_08\n    - power_delta_10\n    - power_delta_14\n    - fpr\n    rows:\n    - - 1\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.048\n    - - 2\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.57\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.033\n    - - 3\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.025\n    - - 4\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.022\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Power curves: detection power vs effect size (δ) for 1-4 cumulative administrations,\n    showing progressive improvement'\n  - 'FPR trajectory: false positive rate decreasing across administrations due to\n    Bayesian shrinkage'\n  analysis: 'Cumulative Bayesian DIF is a natural extension for benchmark monitoring.\n    Under best tier, the power improvement from 0.41 to 0.71 at δ=1.0 after 3 administrations\n    is compelling for longitudinal deployment. Zero GPU cost makes this attractive.\n    However, it''s P2 priority — a nice-to-have for a future-work section or a follow-up\n    paper on operational benchmark monitoring. The simulation is clean and fast to\n    execute; if space permits, a single figure could convey the key message.\n\n    '\n- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement:\n    define validity metrics, simulate replacement rates 5-50%, establish theoretical\n    bounds.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    replacement_5pct_rho: 0.9991\n    replacement_5pct_shr: 0.9928\n    replacement_5pct_construct_coverage: 0.997\n    replacement_10pct_rho: 0.9984\n    replacement_10pct_shr: 0.9921\n    replacement_10pct_construct_coverage: 0.993\n    replacement_20pct_rho: 0.9962\n    replacement_20pct_shr: 0.9904\n    replacement_20pct_construct_coverage: 0.984\n    replacement_50pct_rho: 0.9871\n    replacement_50pct_shr: 0.9842\n    replacement_50pct_construct_coverage: 0.951\n    theoretical_bound_rho: 1 - O(r × (1 - ρ_anchor²))\n    theoretical_bound_shr: 1 - O(r × σ²_replacement / σ²_original)\n    critical_replacement_rate_rho_below_099: 0.27\n    critical_replacement_rate_shr_below_098: 0.41\n  result_rationale: 'MMLU''s massive measurement redundancy (ρ=0.997 at 31% removal\n    from plan_017) means replacement degrades validity very slowly. At 5% replacement,\n    ρ=0.9991 because only 625 items change, and equating ensures replacement items\n    measure similar constructs. The degradation is approximately linear in replacement\n    rate when equating is well- calibrated, because each replaced item introduces\n    independent small perturbation. The theoretical bound involves the replacement\n    rate r multiplied by the (1 - ρ²_anchor) term — when anchor items are strong (ρ_anchor\n    ≈ 0.99), the multiplicative penalty is tiny. SHR degrades slightly faster than\n    Spearman ρ because split-half reliability is more sensitive to local item perturbations\n    than rank ordering. Construct coverage (proportion of subject domains with ≥10\n    items remaining) stays above 0.95 even at 50% replacement because MMLU''s 57 subjects\n    average 219 items each — even halving doesn''t eliminate coverage. The critical\n    rate of 27% for ρ<0.99 aligns with the empirical finding that 31% DIF removal\n    gives ρ=0.996 — replacement with equated items is LESS disruptive than removal.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement'\n    columns:\n    - replacement_pct\n    - spearman_rho\n    - shr\n    - construct_coverage\n    - max_rank_change\n    rows:\n    - - 0\n      - 1.0\n      - 0.9934\n      - 1.0\n      - 0\n    - - 5\n      - 0.9991\n      - 0.9928\n      - 0.997\n      - 18\n    - - 10\n      - 0.9984\n      - 0.9921\n      - 0.993\n      - 42\n    - - 20\n      - 0.9962\n      - 0.9904\n      - 0.984\n      - 78\n    - - 31\n      - 0.9938\n      - 0.9886\n      - 0.972\n      - 127\n    - - 50\n      - 0.9871\n      - 0.9842\n      - 0.951\n      - 203\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: Spearman ρ and SHR vs replacement rate (0-50%), with theoretical bound\n    overlay'\n  - 'Heatmap: per-subject construct coverage at each replacement rate, highlighting\n    subjects dropping below 10 items'\n  analysis: 'This experiment provides the theoretical backbone for equating-based\n    benchmark maintenance. Under best tier, the key message is that MMLU can tolerate\n    ~27% item replacement while maintaining ρ>0.99 — and since DIF flags 31% of items,\n    a single replacement cycle could refresh the most unstable items with minimal\n    validity loss. Depends on plan_005/006; shares their \"future work\" status under\n    current framing.\n\n    '\n- name: item_focused_trees\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) using model release\n    date as continuous covariate. Compare detection power and flexibility vs categorical\n    DIF.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n    n_models_with_release_date: 3842\n    ift_significant_date_effect_pct: 34.7\n    ift_gradual_drift_items_pct: 8.3\n    ift_abrupt_change_items_pct: 26.4\n    ift_categorical_dif_overlap_pct: 81.2\n    ift_unique_detections_pct: 5.8\n    categorical_dif_unique_pct: 2.4\n    ift_split_date_mode: 2023-09\n    ift_split_date_range:\n    - 2023-06\n    - 2024-01\n    ift_detection_power_delta_14: 0.72\n    ift_fpr: 0.041\n    ift_vs_mh_concordance_kappa: 0.74\n  result_rationale: 'IFT treats release date as a continuous covariate and recursively\n    partitions the model sample to find the split that maximizes DIF detection — avoiding\n    the arbitrary 2023/2024-H1 boundary. The 81% overlap with categorical DIF confirms\n    the temporal boundary is approximately correct: IFT''s modal split date (2023-09)\n    is close to the categorical cutoff. The 5.8% unique IFT detections are items with\n    gradual drift — their DIF effect accumulates slowly over time, crossing the threshold\n    only when IFT optimally places the split point. These tend to be items where capability\n    improvement is continuous rather than punctuated (e.g., math reasoning items that\n    gradually become easier). The 8.3% gradual drift items (where IFT fits a smooth\n    function rather than a step change) capture a distinct drift pattern: these items\n    don''t suddenly change but their functioning shifts continuously as model capability\n    increases. IFT detection power (0.72 at δ=1.4) is comparable to MH (0.68) because\n    optimal split placement partially compensates for the loss of predetermined grouping.\n    Cohen''s κ=0.74 indicates substantial agreement — the two methods capture largely\n    overlapping phenomena via different mechanisms.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: IFT vs Categorical DIF Detection Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - significant_pct\n    - unique_pct\n    - fpr\n    - power_delta_14\n    - kappa\n    rows:\n    - - categorical_mh\n      - 31.3\n      - 2.4\n      - 0.042\n      - 0.68\n      - —\n    - - ift_continuous\n      - 34.7\n      - 5.8\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.74\n    - - intersection\n      - 28.9\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n  - title: 'Table: IFT Drift Pattern Classification'\n    columns:\n    - pattern\n    - pct_of_significant\n    - example_domain\n    - interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - abrupt_change\n      - 76.1\n      - abstract_algebra\n      - sudden capability jump at specific model generation\n    - - gradual_drift\n      - 23.9\n      - high_school_mathematics\n      - continuous capability improvement over time\n  figures_description:\n  - 'IFT tree visualization for a representative item: split at 2023-09, showing DIF\n    effect size in each partition'\n  - 'Temporal DIF effect trajectory: 5 example items showing abrupt vs gradual drift\n    patterns'\n  analysis: IFT validates the categorical DIF approach while revealing additional\n    nuance. Under best tier, the 81% overlap confirms the 2023/2024-H1 split is appropriate,\n    and the unique gradual-drift detections provide a richer characterization of temporal\n    instability. P2 priority — an elegant methodological refinement that strengthens\n    Claim 1 but isn't required for it. The key insight (gradual vs abrupt drift patterns)\n    could motivate a follow-up paper on continuous benchmark monitoring. Zero GPU\n    cost; the main investment is implementation of the IFT algorithm.\n","numLines":953,"startLine":1,"totalLines":953}}}
